Pinworm Infection
Paediatric & Neonatal
Nocturnal perianal pruritus, Visible thread-like perianal worms, Positive adhesive tape test

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PDN-040
Reason for Visit: Severe, unrelenting perianal itching disrupting sleep and causing acute distress, requiring evaluation to rule out acute perianal streptococcal infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a highly agitated child screaming from intense anal itching, completely unable to sleep, with parents discovering small white worms in the diaper. Immediate assessment was mandated to rule out painful streptococcal dermatitis, confirm the parasitic infection, and provide immediate anti-helminthic therapy to resolve the acute distress prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "They have been screaming and scratching their bottom all night, and I saw tiny white threads moving in their stool."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with intense, severe perianal pruritus explicitly exacerbating at night, heavily disrupting sleep for the past 72 hours. The parents confirm the visible presence of small, white, thread-like worms strictly around the anus. The child is exhibiting extreme restlessness, frequent waking, and daytime irritability directly caused by the severe nocturnal scratching. There is a complete absence of systemic fever, significant weight loss, bloody diarrhea, or abdominal pain. The child attends daycare and has a known history of poor hand hygiene and thumb-sucking, contributing to fecal-oral autoinoculation.
Functional Status: Highly irritable, profoundly sleep-deprived.
Social/Safety Concerns: Highly contagious transmission to all household members and the daycare cohort.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.7C | RR: 20 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 16 Kg
General: Fidgety, visibly tired, scratching the perianal region.
Physical Exam: Perianal inspection reveals distinct erythema, excoriations, and early secondary bacterial dermatitis strictly in the perianal skin folds. Direct visualization confirms the presence of small (1cm), mobile, white, thread-like worms directly on the perianal skin. The diagnostic tetrad of nocturnal perianal pruritus and visible thread-like perianal worms is explicitly confirmed, rendering the adhesive tape test unnecessary for clinical diagnosis tonight. Abdominal examination is soft and entirely non-tender.
Environmental Observations: Shared bedroom with siblings; toys scattered on the floor.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications. Barrier cream applied with zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Pinworm Infection (Enterobiasis) - Status: Active infection
Differentials:

Perianal Streptococcal Dermatitis - Ruled out explicitly by the direct visualization of live worms and the absence of a sharply demarcated, beefy-red perianal ring with severe pain on defecation.

Candidal Intertrigo - Ruled out by the absence of satellite pustules/papules and the prominent presence of the thread-like worms.

Anal Fissure - Ruled out by the lack of severe, sharp pain during bowel movements, absence of bright red blood on the toilet paper, and the primary complaint of intense pruritus rather than pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Household transmission risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of severe, sleep-depriving perianal pruritus and extreme distress necessitated an immediate after-hours assessment. Telephone triage accurately identified the severe acute discomfort and the need for prompt diagnosis. Objective clinical evaluation conclusively confirmed an active Enterobius vermicularis infection via direct visualization, completely ruling out painful bacterial dermatoses. Delaying this assessment would have subjected the child to another night of agonizing pruritus, worsened the secondary excoriations, and perpetuated the severe sleep deprivation of the entire household. Immediate clinical intervention secured the diagnosis and allowed for the instant administration of anti-helminthic therapy and critical household hygiene education.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Mebendazole 100mg as a single stat dose for the patient AND all household members. Advised application of a mild topical hydrocortisone 1% cream mixed with barrier cream to the perianal excoriations for immediate symptomatic itch relief.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement a strict, visually rewarded morning routine. The child receives a high-value sticker explicitly and strictly only after they successfully complete a thorough handwashing with soap immediately upon waking up and before touching any toys or food. This positively reinforces the exact physical action required to physically break the cycle of fecal-oral autoinoculation, turning hygiene into a game rather than a chore.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review not required unless symptoms persist. A second dose of Mebendazole must be taken in 14 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Routine medical follow-up only. Call the regular GP if the perianal skin becomes hot, exquisitely tender, or starts discharging yellow pus (indicating severe secondary bacterial infection).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Pinworms are highly contagious; re-infection is extremely common without strict environmental hygiene.
Clinical Course: The medication kills the adult worms rapidly, and the itching should subside within 24-48 hours. A second dose is absolutely mandatory in two weeks to kill worms that hatch from surviving eggs.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return to your GP if the skin around the anus becomes bright red, swollen, and highly painful to touch.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The child has swallowed microscopic worm eggs, which hatch in the gut. At night, female worms travel to the outside of the anus to lay eggs, causing the intense itching. The medication kills the worms, but the eggs can survive for weeks in dust, bedding, and on toys.
Actionable Steps: Treat the entire family tonight. Tomorrow morning, wash all bed sheets, pajamas, and towels in hot water. Cut the child's fingernails extremely short to prevent eggs from lodging underneath them during scratching.

